{
  "courseSlug": "understanding-tax-implications-of-salary-structuring",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Chapter 3: Salary Structure Meets the Tax Regime",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "Old Regime Structuring: Getting Full Value From Exemptions",
          "slug": "old-regime-structuring-getting-full-value-from-exemptions",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "k1",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Nisha, an HR business partner in Bengaluru, pays ₹35,000 a month rent, has two school-going children, and plans one family trip a year. She chose the old tax regime because \"HRA and 80C make it better for people like me\". On her company's default salary structure, she paid ₹2,33,950 in tax.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k3",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k4",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "After rebuilding the same ₹20 lakh CTC around her actual life, her tax fell to ₹1,13,180. Nothing illegal, no extra investments beyond what she already made, no change in CTC. The old regime rewards people who structure deliberately, and quietly punishes people who accept whatever structure HR hands them.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "bigIdea",
              "text": "The old regime is not automatically better or worse. It is only as good as the structure you build for it.",
              "_key": "k5"
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "insight",
              "text": "Old regime slabs for tax year 2026-27 are unchanged: nil up to ₹2,50,000, 5% up to ₹5,00,000, 20% up to ₹10,00,000 and 30% above, with a ₹12,500 rebate up to ₹5,00,000 of taxable income and a ₹50,000 standard deduction. Every exemption you add saves tax at these higher rates, which is exactly why structure matters so much more here.",
              "_key": "k6"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k7",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k8",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The old regime toolkit",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "table",
              "caption": "Salary-linked levers in the old regime, tax year 2026-27",
