                    "Other deductions outside salary",
                    "Section 123 (old 80C), Section 126 (old 80D), home loan interest (old Section 24(b)), additional NPS under Section 124 (old 80CCD(1B))",
                    "Plan alongside your structure"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "_key": "k27"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k28",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k29",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Rule 1: Find your HRA sweet spot",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k30",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k31",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "In the old regime, the right HRA component is not \"as high as possible\". Because the exemption is the least of three limits, any HRA above rent minus 10% of basic is simply taxable. The sweet spot is:",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{Ideal HRA} = \\min\\left(\\text{Annual Rent} - 10\\% \\times \\text{Basic},\\; 50\\% \\text{ or } 40\\% \\times \\text{Basic}\\right)",
              "caption": "Anything above this adds nothing to your exemption.",
              "_key": "k32"
            },
            {
              "_type": "table",
              "caption": "Nisha: rent ₹4,20,000 a year, basic ₹10,00,000, Bengaluru (50% city)",
              "headers": [
                "HRA component",
                "Exempt",
                "Taxable HRA"
              ],
              "rows": [
                {
                  "_key": "k34",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "₹2,00,000",
                    "₹2,00,000",
                    "Nil, but ₹1,20,000 of exemption left unused"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k36",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "₹3,20,000",
                    "₹3,20,000",
                    "Nil, the sweet spot"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k38",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "₹4,00,000",
                    "₹3,20,000",
                    "₹80,000"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k40",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "₹5,00,000",
                    "₹3,20,000",
                    "₹1,80,000"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "_key": "k41"
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "warning",
              "text": "Raising basic has a side effect on HRA: it increases the \"10% of basic\" that is knocked off your rent. When Nisha's basic rose from ₹7,00,000 to ₹10,00,000, her rent-minus-10% limit fell from ₹3,50,000 to ₹3,20,000. A higher basic still helped her overall through PF and NPS, but always recheck HRA when basic changes.",
              "_key": "k42"
            },
            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "numericInput",
              "title": "Kabir's sweet spot",
              "prompt": "Kabir works in Jaipur (40% city), is in the old regime, has a basic of ₹6,00,000 a year and pays rent of ₹25,000 a month. What HRA component should he ask for to get the maximum exemption without any taxable HRA?",
              "correctValue": 240000,
              "tolerance": 1,
              "unit": "₹",
              "explanation": "Rent minus 10% of basic = ₹3,00,000 minus ₹60,000 = ₹2,40,000. 40% of basic = ₹2,40,000. The lower of the two is ₹2,40,000, so an HRA component of ₹2,40,000 is fully exempt. Anything more would be taxable.",
              "_key": "k43"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k44",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k45",
                  "_type": "span",
